{"type": "messages", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the isolated synthesizer worker in Research World. Obey the supplied evidence and role boundary. Return one JSON object and no prose."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Synthesize this completed direction into a compact handoff. Explain what was learned, what the experiment or evidence actually supports, what it cannot support, and the most valuable next moves. Never say the overall question is finished. Return {\"title\":str,\"learned\":[str],\"evidence\":[str],\"limitations\":[str],\"open_questions\":[str],\"next_moves\":[str]}.\nINPUT:\n{\"question\": \"The Riemann hypothesis, one of the great unproven problems in mathematics, addresses the distribution of prime numbers. While no pattern of prime numbers is apparent among all natural numbers, G. F. B. Riemann conjectured that their frequency was related to a complex function called the Riemann zeta function, which he used for “for calculating how many primes there are, up to a cutoff, and at what intervals these primes occur, based on the zeroes of the zeta function,” as Frankie Schembri wrote in Science. “However, Riemann’s formula only holds if one assumes that the real parts of these zeta function zeroes are all equal to one-half”—meaning that all the infinitely many nontrivial zeros lie on a straight line equal to one-half. The Riemann hypothesis has been checked for the first 1,000 quintillion solutions.\", \"direction\": {\"title\": \"Proof status and obligations\", \"rationale\": \"Systematically catalog all known equivalences, necessary conditions, and structural barriers that prevent finite computational verification from constituting a mathematical proof.\", \"workflow\": \"proof_boundary\", \"completion_test\": \"A structured dossier mapping every proven equivalence, known barrier class, and explicit demonstration of why empirical zero-counting cannot bridge the infinite gap.\", \"remaining\": \"Human evaluation of whether any mapped barrier suggests a viable axiomatic extension or if the problem requires entirely new foundational tools.\", \"position\": 0}, \"evidence\": {\"sources\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:904e6c045bec741cd71576fd5ba723ef07ebe87dca714ebc538529ae804f7d6d\", \"sha256\": \"904e6c045bec741cd71576fd5ba723ef07ebe87dca714ebc538529ae804f7d6d\", \"title\": \"big list - Collection of equivalent forms of Riemann Hypothesis - MathOverflow\", \"url\": \"https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 1117}, \"content\": \"## big list - Collection of equivalent forms of Riemann Hypothesis - MathOverflow\\n\\n**Source**: https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis\\n\\n---\\n\\n# [Collection of equivalent forms of Riemann Hypothesis](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis)\\n\\n[Ask Question](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/ask)\\n\\nAsked15 years, 10 months ago\\n\\nModified[4 months ago](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis?lastactivity)\\n\\nViewed44k times\\n\\n138[](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/timeline)\\n\\n$\\\\begingroup$\\n\\nThis forum brings together a broad enough base of mathematicians to collect a \\\"big list\\\" of equivalent forms of the Riemann Hypothesis...just for fun. Also,\\nperhaps, this collection could include statements that imply RH or its negation.\\n\\nHere is what I am suggesting we do:\\n\\nConstruct a more or less complete list of sufficiently diverse known reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis and of statements that would resolve the Riemann\\nHypothesis.\\n\\nSince it is in bad taste to directly attack RH, let me provide some rationale for suggesting this:\\n\\n1) The resolution of RH is most likely to require a new point of view or a powerful new approach. It would serve us to collect existing attempts/perspectives in\\na single place in order to reveal new perspectives.\\n\\n2) Perhaps the resolution of RH will need ideas from many areas of mathematics. One hopes that the solution of this problem will exemplify the unity of\\nmathematics, and so it is of interest to see very diverse statements of RH in one place. Even in the event where no solution is near after this effort, the\\nresulting compilation would itself help illustrate the depth of RH.\\n\\n3) It would take very little effort for an expert in a given area to post a favorite known reformulation of RH whose statement is in the language of his area.\\nTherefore, with very little effort, we could have access to many different points of view. This would be a case of many hands making light work. (OK, I guess\\nnot such light work!)\\n\\nAnyhow, in case this indeed turns out to be an appropriate forum for such a collection, you should try to include proper references for any reformulation you\\ninclude.\\n\\n- [big-list](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/tagged/big-list)\\n- [big-picture](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/tagged/big-picture)\\n- [riemann-hypothesis](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/tagged/riemann-hypothesis)\\n\\n[Share](https://mathoverflow.net/q/39944)\\n\\n[Improve this question](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/edit)\\n\\n[editedFeb 29, 2016 at 14:54](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/revisions)\\n\\ncommunity wiki\\n\\n[12 revs, 2 users 100%\\n](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/revisions)[Jon Bannon](https://mathoverflow.net/users/6269)$\\\\endgroup$10\\n\\n-\\n\\n4\\n\\n$\\\\begingroup$You can find a few at:[en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis)$\\\\endgroup$\\n\\nS. Carnahan–[S. Carnahan](https://mathoverflow.net/users/121/s-carnahan)♦\\n\\n2010-09-25 12:41:09 +00:00CommentedSep 25, 2010 at 12:41\\n-\\n\\n$\\\\begingroup$There was an AIM Problem List which included a bunch of examples in connection with last year's \\\"RH Day.\\\" Although the\\nlink:[aimpl.org/pl](http://aimpl.org/pl/)seems not to be working anymore, at least there is this Archive.Org\\nlink:[web.archive.org/web/20120731034246/http://aimath.org/pl/…](https://web.archive.org/web/20120731034246/http://aimath.org/pl/rhequivalences)(hat tip to John\\nWashburn).$\\\\endgroup$\\n\\nMicah Milinovich–[Micah Milinovich](https://mathoverflow.net/users/3659/micah-milinovich)\\n\\n2010-09-25 15:15:51 +00:00CommentedSep 25, 2010 at 15:15\\n-\\n\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:c435a5c8b3d5e4505b90a90478ab4be82af076cc7c4220d073eec0e920c5fc8f\", \"sha256\": \"c435a5c8b3d5e4505b90a90478ab4be82af076cc7c4220d073eec0e920c5fc8f\", \"title\": \"Riemann hypothesis - Wikipedia\", \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 537}, \"content\": \"## Riemann hypothesis - Wikipedia\\n\\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#bodyContent)\\n\\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\\n\\nConjecture on zeros of the zeta function\\n\\nUnsolved problem in mathematics\\n\\nDo all non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function have a real part equal to one half?\\n\\n[More unsolved problems in mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics)\\n\\n[Millennium Prize Problems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems)\\n- [Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_and_Swinnerton-Dyer_conjecture)\\n- [Hodge conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodge_conjecture)\\n- [Navier–Stokes existence and smoothness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navier%E2%80%93Stokes_existence_and_smoothness)\\n- [P versus NP problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P_versus_NP_problem)\\n- [Poincaré conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture)(solved)\\n- [Riemann hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis)\\n- [Yang–Mills existence and mass gap](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yang%E2%80%93Mills_existence_and_mass_gap)\\n\\n- [v](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Millennium_Prize_Problems)\\n- [t](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Millennium_Prize_Problems)\\n- [e](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Millennium_Prize_Problems)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_val\\nue.png)This plot of Riemann's zeta (ζ{\\\\displaystyle \\\\zeta }) function (here with argumentz{\\\\displaystyle z}) shows trivial zeros whereζ(z)=0{\\\\displaystyle \\\\zeta\\n(z)=0}, a pole where*ζ*(*z*) →∞{\\\\displaystyle \\\\infty },the*critical line*of nontrivial zeros\\nwith[Re(*z*)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_numbers#Definition_and_basic_operations)= 1/2 and density of absolute values.\\n\\nIn mathematics, the**Riemann hypothesis**is the[conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conjecture)that the[Riemann zeta\\nfunction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_zeta_function)has its[zeros](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_of_a_function)only at the negative[even\\nintegers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Even_integer)and[complex numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_number)with[real\\npart](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_part)⁠1/2⁠. Many consider it to be the most important[unsolved\\nproblem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics)in[pure\\nmathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pure_mathematics).[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBombieri2000-1)It is of\\ngreat interest in[number theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Number_theory)because it implies results about the distribution of[prime\\nnumbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_numbers). It was proposed by[Bernhard\\nRiemann](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernhard_Riemann),[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#cite_note-2)after whom it is named. According to\\na 2026 survey, there is overwhelming numerical evidence for the hypothesis, but no proof is\\nknown.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#cite_note-3)\\n\\nThe Riemann hypothesis and some of its generalizations, along with[Goldbach's conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldbach's_conjecture)and the[twin prime\\nconjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_prime_conjecture), make up[Hilbert's eighth\\nproblem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert's_eighth_problem)in[David Hilbert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Hilbert)'s list of[twenty-three unsolved\\nproblems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert's_problems); it is also one of the[Millennium Prize\\nProblems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems)of the[Clay Mathematics Institute](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay_Mathematics_Institute),\\nwhich offers[US$](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US$)1 million for a solution to any of them. The name is also used for some closely related analogues, some of\\nwhich have been proved, such as the[Riemann hypothesis for curves over finite\\nfields](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis_for_curves_over_finite_fields), which was proved by[André\\nWeil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Weil).\\n\\nThe Riemann zeta functionζ{\\\\displaystyle \\\\zeta }is\\na[function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_(mathematics))whose[argument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_of_a_function)may be any complex number\\nother than 1, and whose values are also complex. It has zeros at the negative even integers; that is,ζ(s)=0{\\\\displaystyle \\\\zeta (s)=0}whens{\\\\displaystyle s}is\\none of−2,−4,−6,…{\\\\displaystyle -2,-4,-6,\\\\dots }These are called its*trivial zeros*. The zeta function is also zero for other values ofs{\\\\displaystyle s}, which\\nare called*nontrivial zeros*. The Riemann hypothesis is concerned with the locations of these nontrivial zeros, and states that:\\n\\nThe real part of every nontrivial zero of the Riemann zeta function is12{\\\\displaystyle {\\\\frac {1}{2}}}.\\n\\nThus, the hypothesis states that all the nontrivial zeros lie on the*critical line*, consisting of the complex numbers12+it{\\\\displaystyle {\\\\tfrac\\n{1}{2}}+it}wheret{\\\\displaystyle t}is a[real number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_number)andi{\\\\displaystyle i}is the[imaginary\\nunit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imaginary_unit).\\n\\n## Riemann zeta function[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=1)]\\n\\nThe[Riemann zeta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_zeta_function)is defined for complexs{\\\\displaystyle s}with real part greater than 1 by\\nthe[absolutely convergent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_convergence)[infinite\\nseries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinite_series)ζ(s)=∑n=1∞1ns=11s+12s+13s+⋯{\\\\displaystyle \\\\zeta (s)=\\\\sum _{n=1}^{\\\\infty }{\\\\frac {1}{n^{s}}}={\\\\frac\\n{1}{1^{s}}}+{\\\\frac {1}{2^{s}}}+{\\\\frac {1}{3^{s}}}+\\\\cdots }\\n\\n[Leonhard Euler](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonhard_Euler)considered this series in the 1730s for real values ofs{\\\\displaystyle s}, in conjunction with his\\nsolution to the[Basel problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basel_problem). He also proved that it equals the[Euler\\nproduct](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_product)ζ(s)=∏pprime11−p−s=11−2−s⋅11−3−s⋅11−5−s⋅11−7−s⋯{\\\\displaystyle \\\\zeta (s)=\\\\prod _{p{\\\\text{ prime}}}{\\\\frac\\n{1}{1-p^{-s}}}={\\\\frac {1}{1-2^{-s}}}\\\\cdot {\\\\frac {1}{1-3^{-s}}}\\\\cdot {\\\\frac {1}{1-5^{-s}}}\\\\cdot {\\\\frac {1}{1-7^{-s}}}\\\\cdots }\\n\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:5d82f50a6f3928cf623501ddc49e4219e804461b6c603f05bedb5438ef6af263\", \"sha256\": \"5d82f50a6f3928cf623501ddc49e4219e804461b6c603f05bedb5438ef6af263\", \"title\": \"The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\", \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.04022v1\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 506}, \"content\": \"## The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\\n\\n**Source**: https://arxiv.org/html/2602.04022v1\\n\\n---\\n\\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit![Learn more](https://info.arxiv.org/about)\\n\\n[License: CC BY 4.0](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\\n\\narXiv:2602.04022v1 [math.NT] 03 Feb 2026\\n\\n# The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\\n\\n[Alain Connes]\\n\\nAugust 11, 2026\\n\\n###### Abstract\\n\\nThis paper, commissioned as a survey of the Riemann Hypothesis, provides a comprehensive overview of 165 years of mathematical approaches to this fundamental\\nproblem, while introducing a new perspective that emerged during its preparation. The paper begins with a detailed description of what we know about the Riemann\\nzeta function and its zeros, followed by an extensive survey of mathematical theories developed in pursuit of RH—from classical analytic approaches to modern\\ngeometric and physical methods. We also discuss several equivalent formulations of the hypothesis.\\n\\nWithin this survey framework, we present an original contribution in the form of a \\\"Letter to Riemann,\\\" using only mathematics available in his time. This\\nletter reveals a method inspired by Riemann’s own approach to the conformal mapping theorem: by extremizing a quadratic form (restriction of Weil’s quadratic\\nform in modern language), we obtain remarkable approximations to the zeros of zeta. Using only primes less than 13, this optimization procedure yields\\napproximations to the first 50 zeros with accuracies ranging from2.6×10−552.6\\\\times 10^{-55}to10−310^{-3}. Moreover we prove a general result that these\\napproximating values lie exactly on the critical lineℜ⁡(z)=12\\\\Re(z)=\\\\frac{1}{2}.\\n\\nFollowing the letter, we explain the underlying mathematics in modern terms, including the description of a deep connection of the Weil quadratic form with the\\nworld of information theory. The final sections develop a geometric perspective using trace formulas, outlining a potential proof strategy based on establishing\\nconvergence of zeros from finite to infinite Euler products. While completing the commissioned survey, these new results suggest a promising direction for\\nfuture research on Riemann’s conjecture.\\n\\n## 1Introduction\\n\\nThe Riemann Hypothesis (RH) stands as perhaps the most famous unsolved problem in mathematics, having resisted all attempts at proof since Bernhard Riemann\\ncasually remarked in his 1859 memoir that \\\"sehr wahrscheinlich\\\" (very probably) all nontrivial zeros of his zeta function lie on the critical\\nlineℜ⁡(s)=1/2\\\\Re(s)=1/2.\\n\\nThe essence of this paper is a three pages letter to Riemann, written as if it were possible to communicate across the years, and in which I will present a\\nstrategy (towards a proof of RH) which only uses mathematics that he was familiar with, together with the great power of modern computers.\\n\\nIn the section\\\"Encounter with the Riemann zeta function\\\"we aim to convey to the reader a vivid picture of the extraordinary connection that Riemann unveiled\\nbetween the complex zeros of the zeta function and the distribution of prime numbers. We begin with a short historical account of the prime number theorem,\\nfollowed by Riemann’s explicit formula forπ⁡(x)\\\\pi(x), the number of primes belowxx, and its later generalizations into the explicit formulas. From there we\\nturn to one of the most striking consequences: Littlewood’s discovery that the differenceπ⁡(x)−Li⁡(x)\\\\pi(x)-\\\\mathrm{Li}(x)changes sign infinitely often, an\\nobservation that overturned earlier expectations of a simple monotone law.\\nWe then trace the story of what has been established about the zeros ofζ⁡(s)\\\\zeta(s), beginning with Hardy’s proof of their infinitude on the critical line,\\nthrough Selberg’s breakthrough in 1942, and up to modern results concerning the proportion of zeros lying exactly on the critical line. To illuminate this\\ntheory, we also draw on the rich framework of entire functions, including Hadamard’s factorization theorem, Nevanlinna theory, and the remarkable\\nBorchsenius–Jessen theorem, itself a success of the Bohr–Landau program on almost periodic functions.\\nThis part culminates with a result that would surely have amazed Riemann : Voronin’s universality theorem. It asserts that the zeta function, in the\\nstrip1/2<ℜ⁡(s)<11/2<\\\\Re(s)<1, has a chameleon-like property: by vertical translation it can approximate any preassigned functionf⁡(s)f(s)that is continuous,\\nnon-vanishing, and holomorphic on a simply connected compact set within the strip.\\nTaken together, these milestones show both the depth and the subtlety of the analytic structure ofζ⁡(s)\\\\zeta(s). They prepare us to appreciate why the Riemann\\nHypothesis remains so resistant: the function itself embodies an astonishing range of behaviors, far more elusive than any first impression might suggest.\\n\\nRiemann could certainly not have foreseen the extraordinary mathematical landscape that emerged from attempts to prove his conjecture. Entire theories were born\\nfrom this pursuit with connections spanning from algebraic geometry to quantum physics. The section\\\"A century and a Half of Theory Building towards RH\\\"is\\ndevoted to a quick panorama of these theories. The first is the extension of the problem into algebraic and arithmetic geometry. In this setting, the analogue\\nof the Riemann zeta function in finite characteristic was developed, and the corresponding analogue of the Riemann Hypothesis was proved by André Weil. To\\nprovide context, I will briefly recall the notions of Grothendieck schemes and étale cohomology. The essential point here is that Weil’s celebrated “Rosetta\\nstone” analogy becomes clear when viewed through the lens of schemes. In the original Rosetta stone the parallel inscriptions are horizontal: the hieroglyphs at\\nthe top, the Demotic text in the middle, and the Greek at the bottom. Weil proposed a similar three-texts comparison for the Riemann Hypothesis. At the bottom,\\ncorresponding to the Greek text, lies Riemann’s work on Riemann surfaces. There is no analogue of the Riemann Hypothesis in this setting, but it illustrates the\\nextraordinary power of transcendental methods in geometry. The middle text corresponds to algebraic geometry over finite fields: here the analogue of the\\nRiemann Hypothesis was established by Weil, with the zeta function arising as the generating function counting rational points on curves over finite field\\nextensions. Finally, the top one, the “hieroglyphic” text, corresponds to the arithmetic case of the spectrum ofℤ\\\\mathbb{Z}, still deeply mysterious.\\n\\nWhat is remarkable is that, thanks to Grothendieck’s theory of schemes, one sees a unifying framework across all three inscriptions: in each case one is dealing\\nwith regular schemes of dimension one. For this reason, I will briefly evoke schemes, étale cohomology, and motives.\\n\\nAnother major generalization arises from the theory of automorphic forms and representation theory, where the Selberg trace formula and zeta functions play a\\ncentral role.\\n\\nI will then move on to random matrix theory and quantum chaos. A remarkable discovery, initiated by Montgomery and Dyson and later spectacularly confirmed by\\nOdlyzko’s large-scale numerical experiments, is that the pair correlation of the normalized spacings between consecutive zeros ofζ⁡(s)\\\\zeta(s)on the critical\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:1784ec0de0fffc1bd637edf456e996983410a5b4ad64fc5deb9a650b121c557c\", \"sha256\": \"1784ec0de0fffc1bd637edf456e996983410a5b4ad64fc5deb9a650b121c557c\", \"title\": \"some reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis\", \"url\": \"https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/RHreformulations.htm\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 389}, \"content\": \"## some reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis\\n\\n**Source**: https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/RHreformulations.htm\\n\\n---\\n\\n##\\n\\n---\\n\\nsome reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe two classic reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis are \\\"*M*(*x*) = o(*x*1/2 +*a*) for all*a*> 0\\\" ([WWN\\nnotes](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/96a/)) and \\\"*psi*(*x*) -*x*= o(*x*1/2 +*a*) for all*a*> 0\\\" ([WWN\\nnotes](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/95a/)).\\n\\nHere*M*is a step function satisfying*M*(0) = 0, and constant except at positive integers, having a jump of $\\\\mu(n)$ at\\neach*n*.[Recall](https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/ss-k.htm)that the Möbius function $\\\\mu(n)$ is defined to be zero if*n*is divisible by a\\nsquare, and is otherwise equal to (-1)*k*where*k*is the number of distinct prime factors in*n*.\\n\\nThe function*psi*(*x*) is[Chebyshev's prime-counting function](https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/encoding2.htm#chebyshev), which the[Prime\\nNumber Theorem](https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/pnt.htm)tells us is asymptotic to*x*.\\n\\nRecall that the Landau notation*f*(*x*) = o(*g*(*x*)) means that lim*x*-> 0*f*(*x*)/*g*(*x*) = 0.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe following are all notes from[WWN](http://aimath.org/WWN/)'s work-in-progress[\\\"Zeta-functions and associated Riemann Hypotheses\\\"](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/):\\n\\n[Equivalences to Riemann Hypothesis](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/5a/)\\n\\nThe above contains links to abstracts of seven articles which are only accessible to subscribers to MathSciNet. They can be alternatively accessed here:\\n\\nV.V. Volchov,[\\\"On an equality equivalent to the Riemann Hypothesis](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=01410150&format=complete),*Ukr. Math. J.***47**No.3 (1995) 491-493\\n\\nF. Amoroso,[\\\"On the heights of a product of cyclotomic polynomials](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0879.11011&format=complete),*Rend. Semin. Mat. Torino***53**No.3 (1995) 183-191\\n\\nA. Verjovsky,[\\\"Discrete measures and the Riemann hypothesis](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0822.11061&format=complete),*Kodai Math. J.***17**No.3 (1994) 596-608\\n\\nJ. Alcántara-Bode,[\\\"An integral equation formulation of the Riemann hypothesis](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0790.45001&format=complete),*Integral Equations Oper. Theory***17**No.2 (1993) 151-168\\n\\nA. Verjovsky,[\\\"Arithmetic, geometry and dynamics in the unit tangent bundle of the modular orbifold\\\"](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0802.58045&format=complete),*Dyamical Systems. Proceedings of the 3rd international school of\\ndynamical systems, Santiago de Chile, 1990*(R. Bamon,*et.al.*, eds.) Longman Scientific and Tehcnical Pitman Res. Notes Math. Ser 285 (1993) 253-298\\n\\nW. Barrett,*et.al.*,[\\\"On the spectral radius of a (0,1) matrix related to Mertens' function\\\"](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0656.05046&format=complete),*Linear Algebra Appl.***107**(1988) 151-159\\n\\nV.M. Popov,[\\\"On stability properties which are equivalent to Riemann hypothesis\\\"](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0639.34064&format=complete),*Libertas Math.***5**(1985) 55-61\\n\\n[RH equivalence to statement involving error term in Prime Number Theorem](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/28a/)\\n\\n[RH equivalence to statement involving order of*pi*(*x*)-li(*x*)](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/92a/)\\n\\n[M. Riesz's equivalence](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/39a/)from the article \\\"Sur l'hypothèse de Riemann\\\",*Acta Math.***40**(1916) 185-190\\n\\n[Bombieri's refinement of Weil's positivity criterion](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/76a/)\\n\\n[Xian-Jin Li's criterion](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/27a/)\\n\\n[G. Robin's equivalence involving the 'sum of divisors' function](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/61a/)\\n\\n[Nicolas' equivalence involving Euler's totient function](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/99a/)\\n\\n[Massias, Nicolas and Robin's equivalence involving the maximum order of an element in the symmetric group](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/97a/)\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe Riemann Hypothesis can also be reformulated**in terms of a problem involving Farey sequences**. This is dealt with in the following:\\n\\nJ. Franel, \\\"Les suites de Farey et les problemes des nombres premiers.\\\"*Gottinger Nachrichten*, 198-201 (1924)\\n\\nE. Landau, \\\"Bemerkungen zu der vorstehenden Abhandlung von Herrn Franel.\\\"*Gottinger Nachrichten*, 202-206 (1924)\"}], \"summaries\": {\"summaries\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92\", \"summary\": \"A collaborative MathOverflow initiative compiling a comprehensive catalog of known equivalent formulations of the Riemann Hypothesis and related statements. The post advocates for aggregating diverse mathematical perspectives to uncover novel proof strategies, highlight the hypothesis's interdisciplinary depth, and facilitate expert contributions across various subfields.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-1117; scope restricted to community-driven collection of reformulations, implications, and archival references without formal proof attempts.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379\", \"summary\": \"An encyclopedic overview defining the Riemann Hypothesis as the conjecture that all non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function lie on the critical line Re(s)=1/2. It outlines the function's analytic properties, distinguishes trivial from non-trivial zeros, highlights its foundational role in prime number distribution, and notes its status as an unsolved Millennium Prize Problem supported by extensive numerical verification but lacking formal proof.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-537; scope confined to definitional exposition, historical context, Euler product formulation, and established mathematical significance.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86\", \"summary\": \"A comprehensive survey tracing 165 years of analytical, geometric, and physical approaches to the Riemann Hypothesis. It introduces an original method inspired by Riemann’s conformal mapping techniques, utilizing modern computation to extremize a quadratic form for highly accurate zero approximations that provably reside on the critical line. The work contextualizes these findings within algebraic geometry, automorphic forms, and quantum chaos, while proposing a future proof strategy based on trace formulas and the convergence of finite to infinite Euler products.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-506; scope bounded by historical survey, original computational/geometric derivations, and theoretical proof pathways without claiming resolution.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7\", \"summary\": \"A curated reference listing classical and advanced reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis, including bounds on Mertens’ function and Chebyshev’s prime-counting function. It compiles academic equivalences spanning error terms in the Prime Number Theorem, Riesz’s and Li’s criteria, Bombieri’s positivity condition, Robin’s and Nicolas’ number-theoretic inequalities, and dynamical systems approaches involving Farey sequences, providing direct citations to foundational journal articles.\", \"limits\": \"Lines 1-389; scope limited to cataloging specific analytical, arithmetic, and geometric reformulations with academic citations.\"}]}, \"review\": {\"findings\": [{\"check_id\": \"RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92\", \"locator\": \"{\\\"line_start\\\": 1, \\\"line_end\\\": 1117}\", \"sha256\": \"904e6c045bec741cd71576fd5ba723ef07ebe87dca714ebc538529ae804f7d6d\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379\", \"locator\": \"{\\\"line_start\\\": 1, \\\"line_end\\\": 537}\", \"sha256\": \"c435a5c8b3d5e4505b90a90478ab4be82af076cc7c4220d073eec0e920c5fc8f\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86\", \"locator\": \"{\\\"line_start\\\": 1, \\\"line_end\\\": 506}\", \"sha256\": \"5d82f50a6f3928cf623501ddc49e4219e804461b6c603f05bedb5438ef6af263\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7\", \"locator\": \"{\\\"line_start\\\": 1, \\\"line_end\\\": 389}\", \"sha256\": \"1784ec0de0fffc1bd637edf456e996983410a5b4ad64fc5deb9a6501ce6f7\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Replace generic reference materials with targeted theoretical analyses on the limits of empirical verification versus formal proof in analytic number theory, or explicitly frame the current compilation as a preliminary inventory pending rigorous epistemological analysis.\"}], \"summary\": \"The direction mandates an explicit demonstration of the epistemological gap between finite computational verification and infinite mathematical proof regarding the Riemann Hypothesis. However, all provided sources are descriptive catalogs, encyclopedic overviews, or historical surveys that list equivalent formulations without analyzing the structural or logical barriers to empirical proof. Consequently, the artifact set does not support the directional objective, necessitating a major revision of the source material to include rigorous theoretical treatments of verification limits.\"}}, \"claims\": {\"claims\": [{\"node_id\": \"node:051d4df2314b806eb7e85cf5303e38e8\", \"text\": \"Finite computational verification establishes a bound on the first 50 non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function, confirming they reside on the critical line Re(s)=1/2 with approximation errors not exceeding 10^-3, yet this empirical data cannot bridge the infinite gap to constitute a formal proof.\", \"kind\": \"proof_boundary\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86\", \"source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379\"], \"scope\": \"analytic number theory / computational verification limits\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 0, \"challenge\": \"Mischaracterizes Connes' quadratic form optimization as direct computational verification and conflates high-precision numerical candidates with rigorous analytic bounds. The 'infinite gap' is not merely a matter of finite versus infinite enumeration but stems from the absence of uniform analytic control over the critical strip at arbitrary heights.\", \"repair\": \"Reframe to specify that numerical approximations yield high-accuracy candidates on the critical line but cannot substitute for analytic continuation arguments required to exclude off-line zeros universally, emphasizing the necessity of uniform bounds across all imaginary parts rather than discrete verification.\"}}, {\"node_id\": \"node:33b01e21dc5e6aa026ac18fa109ca893\", \"text\": \"The Riemann Hypothesis remains an unresolved mathematical question because the structural barrier between finite Euler product convergence and infinite analytic continuation prevents any single equivalent formulation—spanning algebraic geometry, automorphic forms, or random matrix theory—from yielding a definitive proof without entirely new foundational tools.\", \"kind\": \"proof_boundary\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92\", \"source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86\"], \"scope\": \"mathematical logic / proof theory\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 1, \"challenge\": \"Overstates the necessity of 'entirely new foundational tools.' Established frameworks like trace formulas, automorphic forms, and spectral theory are mature but lack a proven self-adjoint operator whose spectrum matches the zeta zeros. The barrier is structural (missing spectral correspondence) rather than purely logical or axiomatic.\", \"repair\": \"Clarify that resolution hinges on establishing a concrete spectral interpretation within existing geometric and analytic frameworks, focusing on constructing operators with the correct eigenvalue distribution and refining trace formula applications instead of invoking new axioms or disjoint foundational systems.\"}}, {\"node_id\": \"node:ec56dd793c9a110d98c698283bdf7c70\", \"text\": \"Any viable axiomatic extension proposed to resolve the Riemann Hypothesis must satisfy a strict bound on prime-counting error terms, specifically requiring that psi(x) - x = o(x^(1/2+epsilon)) holds uniformly, as current reformulations tie the hypothesis's validity directly to these asymptotic constraints rather than finite case enumeration.\", \"kind\": \"bound\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7\"], \"scope\": \"number theory / asymptotic analysis\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 2, \"challenge\": \"Incorrectly posits that resolving RH requires an 'axiomatic extension,' whereas RH is widely expected to be provable within standard ZFC set theory. The equivalence involving psi(x) is a classical reformulation, not a foundational barrier, and framing it as such misdirects efforts toward unnecessary set-theoretic additions rather than analytic refinements.\", \"repair\": \"Replace 'axiomatic extension' with 'analytic refinement,' specifying that progress requires leveraging existing equivalences to derive uniform error bounds through trace formulas or modular forms within standard arithmetic geometry, avoiding speculative foundational shifts while targeting precise asymptotic control.\"}}], \"rebuttal\": {\"rebuttals\": [{\"claim_index\": 0, \"challenge\": \"Mischaracterizes Connes' quadratic form optimization as direct computational verification and conflates high-precision numerical candidates with rigorous analytic bounds. The 'infinite gap' is not merely a matter of finite versus infinite enumeration but stems from the absence of uniform analytic control over the critical strip at arbitrary heights.\", \"repair\": \"Reframe to specify that numerical approximations yield high-accuracy candidates on the critical line but cannot substitute for analytic continuation arguments required to exclude off-line zeros universally, emphasizing the necessity of uniform bounds across all imaginary parts rather than discrete verification.\"}, {\"claim_index\": 1, \"challenge\": \"Overstates the necessity of 'entirely new foundational tools.' Established frameworks like trace formulas, automorphic forms, and spectral theory are mature but lack a proven self-adjoint operator whose spectrum matches the zeta zeros. The barrier is structural (missing spectral correspondence) rather than purely logical or axiomatic.\", \"repair\": \"Clarify that resolution hinges on establishing a concrete spectral interpretation within existing geometric and analytic frameworks, focusing on constructing operators with the correct eigenvalue distribution and refining trace formula applications instead of invoking new axioms or disjoint foundational systems.\"}, {\"claim_index\": 2, \"challenge\": \"Incorrectly posits that resolving RH requires an 'axiomatic extension,' whereas RH is widely expected to be provable within standard ZFC set theory. The equivalence involving psi(x) is a classical reformulation, not a foundational barrier, and framing it as such misdirects efforts toward unnecessary set-theoretic additions rather than analytic refinements.\", \"repair\": \"Replace 'axiomatic extension' with 'analytic refinement,' specifying that progress requires leveraging existing equivalences to derive uniform error bounds through trace formulas or modular forms within standard arithmetic geometry, avoiding speculative foundational shifts while targeting precise asymptotic control.\"}]}, \"review\": {\"findings\": [{\"check_id\": \"PROOF-OBLIGATION-GAP\", \"claim_index\": 0, \"severity\": \"major\", \"analysis\": \"Fails to specify the precise analytic obligation required to bridge the empirical-to-formal gap. The claim reduces the 'infinite gap' to a matter of finite versus infinite enumeration, omitting the necessity of uniform analytic bounds across all imaginary parts to exclude off-line zeros universally.\", \"remediation\": \"Reframe to emphasize that high-precision numerical candidates cannot substitute for analytic continuation arguments, explicitly stating the obligation to derive uniform bounds via trace formulas or modular forms.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"THEOREM-SCOPE-DRIFT\", \"claim_index\": 1, \"severity\": \"major\", \"analysis\": \"Drifts from established analytic number theory into speculative foundational territory by asserting that 'entirely new foundational tools' are necessary. The actual barrier is structural (missing spectral correspondence/self-adjoint operators), not axiomatic or logical.\", \"remediation\": \"Restrict scope to existing geometric and analytic frameworks. Clarify that resolution hinges on constructing concrete spectral interpretations and refining trace formula applications within mature theories like automorphic forms.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"ASSUMPTION-SMUGGLE\", \"claim_index\": 1, \"severity\": \"moderate\", \"analysis\": \"Smuggles the assumption that equivalent formulations function as isolated structural barriers preventing proof. Logically, equivalent statements share identical truth conditions; proving one necessitates proving all, making the framing of 'single equivalent formulation' barriers logically incoherent.\", \"remediation\": \"Replace barrier language with logical interchangeability. Focus analysis on why current analytic techniques fail to establish the required uniform estimates rather than attributing failure to isolated reformulations.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"ASSUMPTION-SMUGGLE\", \"claim_index\": 2, \"severity\": \"major\", \"analysis\": \"Illegitimately assumes that resolving RH requires an 'axiomatic extension,' contradicting the overwhelming consensus that the hypothesis is resolvable within standard ZFC set theory. Misframes a classical asymptotic equivalence as a foundational prerequisite.\", \"remediation\": \"Replace 'axiomatic extension' with 'analytic refinement.' Specify that progress requires leveraging existing equivalences to derive uniform error bounds through trace formulas or modular forms without invoking speculative set-theoretic additions.\"}]}}, \"experiment\": null}"}]}
{"type": "model_output", "text": "{\n  \"title\": \"Riemann Hypothesis Proof Boundaries and Structural Barriers\",\n  \"learned\": [\n    \"The Riemann Hypothesis encompasses a dense network of mathematically equivalent formulations spanning analytic number theory, algebraic geometry, and spectral theory.\",\n    \"High-precision numerical approximations confirm zeros reside on the critical line but cannot substitute for the uniform analytic bounds required to exclude off-line zeros universally.\",\n    \"The core obstacle to resolution is structural—specifically the absence of a proven self-adjoint operator whose spectrum matches the zeta zeros—rather than axiomatic or purely logical.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    \"Comprehensive catalogs of equivalent statements including Mertens function bounds, Chebyshev psi error terms, Robin's and Nicolas' inequalities, and Farey sequence properties.\",\n    \"Historical and theoretical surveys detailing 165 years of approaches, including Connes' quadratic form optimization yielding highly accurate critical-line candidates.\",\n    \"Established mathematical connections between zeta zeros, prime distribution error terms, automorphic forms, and random matrix theory pair correlations.\"\n  ],\n  \"limitations\": [\n    \"Provided sources are descriptive compilations and surveys lacking rigorous epistemological analysis of why finite verification fails to constitute formal proof.\",\n    \"Cannot demonstrate that empirical zero-counting bridges the infinite gap, as the gap stems from missing uniform analytic control across arbitrary heights in the critical strip.\",\n    \"Does not support claims requiring new axiomatic extensions, as the hypothesis is widely expected to be resolvable within standard ZFC set theory using existing frameworks.\"\n  ],\n  \"open_questions\": [\n    \"How can a concrete self-adjoint operator be constructed within existing geometric or analytic frameworks to match the nontrivial zeta zeros?\",\n    \"Which refinement of trace formulas or modular forms will yield the necessary uniform error bounds for prime-counting functions across all imaginary parts?\",\n    \"How do the diverse equivalent formulations structurally interlock to reveal a unified proof pathway without relying on speculative foundational shifts?\"\n  ],\n  \"next_moves\": [\n    \"Replace broad equivalence catalogs with targeted theoretical analyses focusing on spectral correspondence and trace formula convergence mechanisms.\",\n    \"Develop rigorous analytic arguments establishing uniform bounds on prime-counting error terms within standard arithmetic geometry.\",\n    \"Investigate the construction of operators with correct eigenvalue distributions using mature theories like automorphic forms and étale cohomology.\",\n    \"Evaluate specific classical reformulations (e.g., Li's criterion, Weil's positivity) for actionable structural insights rather than treating them as isolated proof barriers.\"\n  ]\n}", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 11500, "total_tokens": 14060, "completion_tokens": 2560, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 2002, "text_tokens": 2560}}, "model": "qwen3.7-flash"}
{"type": "attempt_input", "content": {"input": {"question": "The Riemann hypothesis, one of the great unproven problems in mathematics, addresses the distribution of prime numbers. While no pattern of prime numbers is apparent among all natural numbers, G. F. B. Riemann conjectured that their frequency was related to a complex function called the Riemann zeta function, which he used for “for calculating how many primes there are, up to a cutoff, and at what intervals these primes occur, based on the zeroes of the zeta function,” as Frankie Schembri wrote in Science. “However, Riemann’s formula only holds if one assumes that the real parts of these zeta function zeroes are all equal to one-half”—meaning that all the infinitely many nontrivial zeros lie on a straight line equal to one-half. The Riemann hypothesis has been checked for the first 1,000 quintillion solutions.", "direction": {"title": "Proof status and obligations", "rationale": "Systematically catalog all known equivalences, necessary conditions, and structural barriers that prevent finite computational verification from constituting a mathematical proof.", "workflow": "proof_boundary", "completion_test": "A structured dossier mapping every proven equivalence, known barrier class, and explicit demonstration of why empirical zero-counting cannot bridge the infinite gap.", "remaining": "Human evaluation of whether any mapped barrier suggests a viable axiomatic extension or if the problem requires entirely new foundational tools.", "position": 0}, "evidence": {"sources": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92", "artifact_id": "artifact:904e6c045bec741cd71576fd5ba723ef07ebe87dca714ebc538529ae804f7d6d", "sha256": "904e6c045bec741cd71576fd5ba723ef07ebe87dca714ebc538529ae804f7d6d", "title": "big list - Collection of equivalent forms of Riemann Hypothesis - MathOverflow", "url": "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 1117}, "content": "## big list - Collection of equivalent forms of Riemann Hypothesis - MathOverflow\n\n**Source**: https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis\n\n---\n\n# [Collection of equivalent forms of Riemann Hypothesis](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis)\n\n[Ask Question](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/ask)\n\nAsked15 years, 10 months ago\n\nModified[4 months ago](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/39944/collection-of-equivalent-forms-of-riemann-hypothesis?lastactivity)\n\nViewed44k times\n\n138[](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/timeline)\n\n$\\begingroup$\n\nThis forum brings together a broad enough base of mathematicians to collect a \"big list\" of equivalent forms of the Riemann Hypothesis...just for fun. Also,\nperhaps, this collection could include statements that imply RH or its negation.\n\nHere is what I am suggesting we do:\n\nConstruct a more or less complete list of sufficiently diverse known reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis and of statements that would resolve the Riemann\nHypothesis.\n\nSince it is in bad taste to directly attack RH, let me provide some rationale for suggesting this:\n\n1) The resolution of RH is most likely to require a new point of view or a powerful new approach. It would serve us to collect existing attempts/perspectives in\na single place in order to reveal new perspectives.\n\n2) Perhaps the resolution of RH will need ideas from many areas of mathematics. One hopes that the solution of this problem will exemplify the unity of\nmathematics, and so it is of interest to see very diverse statements of RH in one place. Even in the event where no solution is near after this effort, the\nresulting compilation would itself help illustrate the depth of RH.\n\n3) It would take very little effort for an expert in a given area to post a favorite known reformulation of RH whose statement is in the language of his area.\nTherefore, with very little effort, we could have access to many different points of view. This would be a case of many hands making light work. (OK, I guess\nnot such light work!)\n\nAnyhow, in case this indeed turns out to be an appropriate forum for such a collection, you should try to include proper references for any reformulation you\ninclude.\n\n- [big-list](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/tagged/big-list)\n- [big-picture](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/tagged/big-picture)\n- [riemann-hypothesis](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/tagged/riemann-hypothesis)\n\n[Share](https://mathoverflow.net/q/39944)\n\n[Improve this question](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/edit)\n\n[editedFeb 29, 2016 at 14:54](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/revisions)\n\ncommunity wiki\n\n[12 revs, 2 users 100%\n](https://mathoverflow.net/posts/39944/revisions)[Jon Bannon](https://mathoverflow.net/users/6269)$\\endgroup$10\n\n-\n\n4\n\n$\\begingroup$You can find a few at:[en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis)$\\endgroup$\n\nS. Carnahan–[S. Carnahan](https://mathoverflow.net/users/121/s-carnahan)♦\n\n2010-09-25 12:41:09 +00:00CommentedSep 25, 2010 at 12:41\n-\n\n$\\begingroup$There was an AIM Problem List which included a bunch of examples in connection with last year's \"RH Day.\" Although the\nlink:[aimpl.org/pl](http://aimpl.org/pl/)seems not to be working anymore, at least there is this Archive.Org\nlink:[web.archive.org/web/20120731034246/http://aimath.org/pl/…](https://web.archive.org/web/20120731034246/http://aimath.org/pl/rhequivalences)(hat tip to John\nWashburn).$\\endgroup$\n\nMicah Milinovich–[Micah Milinovich](https://mathoverflow.net/users/3659/micah-milinovich)\n\n2010-09-25 15:15:51 +00:00CommentedSep 25, 2010 at 15:15\n-\n"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379", "artifact_id": "artifact:c435a5c8b3d5e4505b90a90478ab4be82af076cc7c4220d073eec0e920c5fc8f", "sha256": "c435a5c8b3d5e4505b90a90478ab4be82af076cc7c4220d073eec0e920c5fc8f", "title": "Riemann hypothesis - Wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 537}, "content": "## Riemann hypothesis - Wikipedia\n\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis\n\n---\n\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#bodyContent)\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nConjecture on zeros of the zeta function\n\nUnsolved problem in mathematics\n\nDo all non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function have a real part equal to one half?\n\n[More unsolved problems in mathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics)\n\n[Millennium Prize Problems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems)\n- [Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_and_Swinnerton-Dyer_conjecture)\n- [Hodge conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodge_conjecture)\n- [Navier–Stokes existence and smoothness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navier%E2%80%93Stokes_existence_and_smoothness)\n- [P versus NP problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P_versus_NP_problem)\n- [Poincaré conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture)(solved)\n- [Riemann hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis)\n- [Yang–Mills existence and mass gap](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yang%E2%80%93Mills_existence_and_mass_gap)\n\n- [v](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Millennium_Prize_Problems)\n- [t](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Millennium_Prize_Problems)\n- [e](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Millennium_Prize_Problems)[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Riemann_zeta_function_absolute_val\nue.png)This plot of Riemann's zeta (ζ{\\displaystyle \\zeta }) function (here with argumentz{\\displaystyle z}) shows trivial zeros whereζ(z)=0{\\displaystyle \\zeta\n(z)=0}, a pole where*ζ*(*z*) →∞{\\displaystyle \\infty },the*critical line*of nontrivial zeros\nwith[Re(*z*)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_numbers#Definition_and_basic_operations)= 1/2 and density of absolute values.\n\nIn mathematics, the**Riemann hypothesis**is the[conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conjecture)that the[Riemann zeta\nfunction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_zeta_function)has its[zeros](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_of_a_function)only at the negative[even\nintegers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Even_integer)and[complex numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_number)with[real\npart](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_part)⁠1/2⁠. Many consider it to be the most important[unsolved\nproblem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics)in[pure\nmathematics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pure_mathematics).[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBombieri2000-1)It is of\ngreat interest in[number theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Number_theory)because it implies results about the distribution of[prime\nnumbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_numbers). It was proposed by[Bernhard\nRiemann](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernhard_Riemann),[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#cite_note-2)after whom it is named. According to\na 2026 survey, there is overwhelming numerical evidence for the hypothesis, but no proof is\nknown.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis#cite_note-3)\n\nThe Riemann hypothesis and some of its generalizations, along with[Goldbach's conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldbach's_conjecture)and the[twin prime\nconjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_prime_conjecture), make up[Hilbert's eighth\nproblem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert's_eighth_problem)in[David Hilbert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Hilbert)'s list of[twenty-three unsolved\nproblems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert's_problems); it is also one of the[Millennium Prize\nProblems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems)of the[Clay Mathematics Institute](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay_Mathematics_Institute),\nwhich offers[US$](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US$)1 million for a solution to any of them. The name is also used for some closely related analogues, some of\nwhich have been proved, such as the[Riemann hypothesis for curves over finite\nfields](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis_for_curves_over_finite_fields), which was proved by[André\nWeil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Weil).\n\nThe Riemann zeta functionζ{\\displaystyle \\zeta }is\na[function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_(mathematics))whose[argument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_of_a_function)may be any complex number\nother than 1, and whose values are also complex. It has zeros at the negative even integers; that is,ζ(s)=0{\\displaystyle \\zeta (s)=0}whens{\\displaystyle s}is\none of−2,−4,−6,…{\\displaystyle -2,-4,-6,\\dots }These are called its*trivial zeros*. The zeta function is also zero for other values ofs{\\displaystyle s}, which\nare called*nontrivial zeros*. The Riemann hypothesis is concerned with the locations of these nontrivial zeros, and states that:\n\nThe real part of every nontrivial zero of the Riemann zeta function is12{\\displaystyle {\\frac {1}{2}}}.\n\nThus, the hypothesis states that all the nontrivial zeros lie on the*critical line*, consisting of the complex numbers12+it{\\displaystyle {\\tfrac\n{1}{2}}+it}wheret{\\displaystyle t}is a[real number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_number)andi{\\displaystyle i}is the[imaginary\nunit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imaginary_unit).\n\n## Riemann zeta function[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Riemann_hypothesis&action=edit&section=1)]\n\nThe[Riemann zeta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_zeta_function)is defined for complexs{\\displaystyle s}with real part greater than 1 by\nthe[absolutely convergent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_convergence)[infinite\nseries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinite_series)ζ(s)=∑n=1∞1ns=11s+12s+13s+⋯{\\displaystyle \\zeta (s)=\\sum _{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\frac {1}{n^{s}}}={\\frac\n{1}{1^{s}}}+{\\frac {1}{2^{s}}}+{\\frac {1}{3^{s}}}+\\cdots }\n\n[Leonhard Euler](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonhard_Euler)considered this series in the 1730s for real values ofs{\\displaystyle s}, in conjunction with his\nsolution to the[Basel problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basel_problem). He also proved that it equals the[Euler\nproduct](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_product)ζ(s)=∏pprime11−p−s=11−2−s⋅11−3−s⋅11−5−s⋅11−7−s⋯{\\displaystyle \\zeta (s)=\\prod _{p{\\text{ prime}}}{\\frac\n{1}{1-p^{-s}}}={\\frac {1}{1-2^{-s}}}\\cdot {\\frac {1}{1-3^{-s}}}\\cdot {\\frac {1}{1-5^{-s}}}\\cdot {\\frac {1}{1-7^{-s}}}\\cdots }\n"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86", "artifact_id": "artifact:5d82f50a6f3928cf623501ddc49e4219e804461b6c603f05bedb5438ef6af263", "sha256": "5d82f50a6f3928cf623501ddc49e4219e804461b6c603f05bedb5438ef6af263", "title": "The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2602.04022v1", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 506}, "content": "## The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\n\n**Source**: https://arxiv.org/html/2602.04022v1\n\n---\n\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit![Learn more](https://info.arxiv.org/about)\n\n[License: CC BY 4.0](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n\narXiv:2602.04022v1 [math.NT] 03 Feb 2026\n\n# The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time\n\n[Alain Connes]\n\nAugust 11, 2026\n\n###### Abstract\n\nThis paper, commissioned as a survey of the Riemann Hypothesis, provides a comprehensive overview of 165 years of mathematical approaches to this fundamental\nproblem, while introducing a new perspective that emerged during its preparation. The paper begins with a detailed description of what we know about the Riemann\nzeta function and its zeros, followed by an extensive survey of mathematical theories developed in pursuit of RH—from classical analytic approaches to modern\ngeometric and physical methods. We also discuss several equivalent formulations of the hypothesis.\n\nWithin this survey framework, we present an original contribution in the form of a \"Letter to Riemann,\" using only mathematics available in his time. This\nletter reveals a method inspired by Riemann’s own approach to the conformal mapping theorem: by extremizing a quadratic form (restriction of Weil’s quadratic\nform in modern language), we obtain remarkable approximations to the zeros of zeta. Using only primes less than 13, this optimization procedure yields\napproximations to the first 50 zeros with accuracies ranging from2.6×10−552.6\\times 10^{-55}to10−310^{-3}. Moreover we prove a general result that these\napproximating values lie exactly on the critical lineℜ⁡(z)=12\\Re(z)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n\nFollowing the letter, we explain the underlying mathematics in modern terms, including the description of a deep connection of the Weil quadratic form with the\nworld of information theory. The final sections develop a geometric perspective using trace formulas, outlining a potential proof strategy based on establishing\nconvergence of zeros from finite to infinite Euler products. While completing the commissioned survey, these new results suggest a promising direction for\nfuture research on Riemann’s conjecture.\n\n## 1Introduction\n\nThe Riemann Hypothesis (RH) stands as perhaps the most famous unsolved problem in mathematics, having resisted all attempts at proof since Bernhard Riemann\ncasually remarked in his 1859 memoir that \"sehr wahrscheinlich\" (very probably) all nontrivial zeros of his zeta function lie on the critical\nlineℜ⁡(s)=1/2\\Re(s)=1/2.\n\nThe essence of this paper is a three pages letter to Riemann, written as if it were possible to communicate across the years, and in which I will present a\nstrategy (towards a proof of RH) which only uses mathematics that he was familiar with, together with the great power of modern computers.\n\nIn the section\"Encounter with the Riemann zeta function\"we aim to convey to the reader a vivid picture of the extraordinary connection that Riemann unveiled\nbetween the complex zeros of the zeta function and the distribution of prime numbers. We begin with a short historical account of the prime number theorem,\nfollowed by Riemann’s explicit formula forπ⁡(x)\\pi(x), the number of primes belowxx, and its later generalizations into the explicit formulas. From there we\nturn to one of the most striking consequences: Littlewood’s discovery that the differenceπ⁡(x)−Li⁡(x)\\pi(x)-\\mathrm{Li}(x)changes sign infinitely often, an\nobservation that overturned earlier expectations of a simple monotone law.\nWe then trace the story of what has been established about the zeros ofζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s), beginning with Hardy’s proof of their infinitude on the critical line,\nthrough Selberg’s breakthrough in 1942, and up to modern results concerning the proportion of zeros lying exactly on the critical line. To illuminate this\ntheory, we also draw on the rich framework of entire functions, including Hadamard’s factorization theorem, Nevanlinna theory, and the remarkable\nBorchsenius–Jessen theorem, itself a success of the Bohr–Landau program on almost periodic functions.\nThis part culminates with a result that would surely have amazed Riemann : Voronin’s universality theorem. It asserts that the zeta function, in the\nstrip1/2<ℜ⁡(s)<11/2<\\Re(s)<1, has a chameleon-like property: by vertical translation it can approximate any preassigned functionf⁡(s)f(s)that is continuous,\nnon-vanishing, and holomorphic on a simply connected compact set within the strip.\nTaken together, these milestones show both the depth and the subtlety of the analytic structure ofζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s). They prepare us to appreciate why the Riemann\nHypothesis remains so resistant: the function itself embodies an astonishing range of behaviors, far more elusive than any first impression might suggest.\n\nRiemann could certainly not have foreseen the extraordinary mathematical landscape that emerged from attempts to prove his conjecture. Entire theories were born\nfrom this pursuit with connections spanning from algebraic geometry to quantum physics. The section\"A century and a Half of Theory Building towards RH\"is\ndevoted to a quick panorama of these theories. The first is the extension of the problem into algebraic and arithmetic geometry. In this setting, the analogue\nof the Riemann zeta function in finite characteristic was developed, and the corresponding analogue of the Riemann Hypothesis was proved by André Weil. To\nprovide context, I will briefly recall the notions of Grothendieck schemes and étale cohomology. The essential point here is that Weil’s celebrated “Rosetta\nstone” analogy becomes clear when viewed through the lens of schemes. In the original Rosetta stone the parallel inscriptions are horizontal: the hieroglyphs at\nthe top, the Demotic text in the middle, and the Greek at the bottom. Weil proposed a similar three-texts comparison for the Riemann Hypothesis. At the bottom,\ncorresponding to the Greek text, lies Riemann’s work on Riemann surfaces. There is no analogue of the Riemann Hypothesis in this setting, but it illustrates the\nextraordinary power of transcendental methods in geometry. The middle text corresponds to algebraic geometry over finite fields: here the analogue of the\nRiemann Hypothesis was established by Weil, with the zeta function arising as the generating function counting rational points on curves over finite field\nextensions. Finally, the top one, the “hieroglyphic” text, corresponds to the arithmetic case of the spectrum ofℤ\\mathbb{Z}, still deeply mysterious.\n\nWhat is remarkable is that, thanks to Grothendieck’s theory of schemes, one sees a unifying framework across all three inscriptions: in each case one is dealing\nwith regular schemes of dimension one. For this reason, I will briefly evoke schemes, étale cohomology, and motives.\n\nAnother major generalization arises from the theory of automorphic forms and representation theory, where the Selberg trace formula and zeta functions play a\ncentral role.\n\nI will then move on to random matrix theory and quantum chaos. A remarkable discovery, initiated by Montgomery and Dyson and later spectacularly confirmed by\nOdlyzko’s large-scale numerical experiments, is that the pair correlation of the normalized spacings between consecutive zeros ofζ⁡(s)\\zeta(s)on the critical"}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7", "artifact_id": "artifact:1784ec0de0fffc1bd637edf456e996983410a5b4ad64fc5deb9a650b121c557c", "sha256": "1784ec0de0fffc1bd637edf456e996983410a5b4ad64fc5deb9a650b121c557c", "title": "some reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis", "url": "https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/RHreformulations.htm", "locator": {"line_start": 1, "line_end": 389}, "content": "## some reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis\n\n**Source**: https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/RHreformulations.htm\n\n---\n\n##\n\n---\n\nsome reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis\n\n---\n\nThe two classic reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis are \"*M*(*x*) = o(*x*1/2 +*a*) for all*a*> 0\" ([WWN\nnotes](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/96a/)) and \"*psi*(*x*) -*x*= o(*x*1/2 +*a*) for all*a*> 0\" ([WWN\nnotes](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/95a/)).\n\nHere*M*is a step function satisfying*M*(0) = 0, and constant except at positive integers, having a jump of $\\mu(n)$ at\neach*n*.[Recall](https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/ss-k.htm)that the Möbius function $\\mu(n)$ is defined to be zero if*n*is divisible by a\nsquare, and is otherwise equal to (-1)*k*where*k*is the number of distinct prime factors in*n*.\n\nThe function*psi*(*x*) is[Chebyshev's prime-counting function](https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/encoding2.htm#chebyshev), which the[Prime\nNumber Theorem](https://empslocal.ex.ac.uk/people/staff/mrwatkin/zeta/pnt.htm)tells us is asymptotic to*x*.\n\nRecall that the Landau notation*f*(*x*) = o(*g*(*x*)) means that lim*x*-> 0*f*(*x*)/*g*(*x*) = 0.\n\n---\n\nThe following are all notes from[WWN](http://aimath.org/WWN/)'s work-in-progress[\"Zeta-functions and associated Riemann Hypotheses\"](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/):\n\n[Equivalences to Riemann Hypothesis](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/5a/)\n\nThe above contains links to abstracts of seven articles which are only accessible to subscribers to MathSciNet. They can be alternatively accessed here:\n\nV.V. Volchov,[\"On an equality equivalent to the Riemann Hypothesis](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=01410150&format=complete),*Ukr. Math. J.***47**No.3 (1995) 491-493\n\nF. Amoroso,[\"On the heights of a product of cyclotomic polynomials](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0879.11011&format=complete),*Rend. Semin. Mat. Torino***53**No.3 (1995) 183-191\n\nA. Verjovsky,[\"Discrete measures and the Riemann hypothesis](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0822.11061&format=complete),*Kodai Math. J.***17**No.3 (1994) 596-608\n\nJ. Alcántara-Bode,[\"An integral equation formulation of the Riemann hypothesis](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0790.45001&format=complete),*Integral Equations Oper. Theory***17**No.2 (1993) 151-168\n\nA. Verjovsky,[\"Arithmetic, geometry and dynamics in the unit tangent bundle of the modular orbifold\"](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0802.58045&format=complete),*Dyamical Systems. Proceedings of the 3rd international school of\ndynamical systems, Santiago de Chile, 1990*(R. Bamon,*et.al.*, eds.) Longman Scientific and Tehcnical Pitman Res. Notes Math. Ser 285 (1993) 253-298\n\nW. Barrett,*et.al.*,[\"On the spectral radius of a (0,1) matrix related to Mertens' function\"](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0656.05046&format=complete),*Linear Algebra Appl.***107**(1988) 151-159\n\nV.M. Popov,[\"On stability properties which are equivalent to Riemann hypothesis\"](http://www.emis.de/cgi-\nbin/zmen/ZMATH/en/quick.html?first=1&maxdocs=20&type=html&an=0639.34064&format=complete),*Libertas Math.***5**(1985) 55-61\n\n[RH equivalence to statement involving error term in Prime Number Theorem](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/28a/)\n\n[RH equivalence to statement involving order of*pi*(*x*)-li(*x*)](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/92a/)\n\n[M. Riesz's equivalence](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/39a/)from the article \"Sur l'hypothèse de Riemann\",*Acta Math.***40**(1916) 185-190\n\n[Bombieri's refinement of Weil's positivity criterion](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/76a/)\n\n[Xian-Jin Li's criterion](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/27a/)\n\n[G. Robin's equivalence involving the 'sum of divisors' function](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/61a/)\n\n[Nicolas' equivalence involving Euler's totient function](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/99a/)\n\n[Massias, Nicolas and Robin's equivalence involving the maximum order of an element in the symmetric group](http://aimath.org/WWN/rh/articles/html/97a/)\n\n---\n\nThe Riemann Hypothesis can also be reformulated**in terms of a problem involving Farey sequences**. This is dealt with in the following:\n\nJ. Franel, \"Les suites de Farey et les problemes des nombres premiers.\"*Gottinger Nachrichten*, 198-201 (1924)\n\nE. Landau, \"Bemerkungen zu der vorstehenden Abhandlung von Herrn Franel.\"*Gottinger Nachrichten*, 202-206 (1924)"}], "summaries": {"summaries": [{"source_id": "source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92", "summary": "A collaborative MathOverflow initiative compiling a comprehensive catalog of known equivalent formulations of the Riemann Hypothesis and related statements. The post advocates for aggregating diverse mathematical perspectives to uncover novel proof strategies, highlight the hypothesis's interdisciplinary depth, and facilitate expert contributions across various subfields.", "limits": "Lines 1-1117; scope restricted to community-driven collection of reformulations, implications, and archival references without formal proof attempts."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379", "summary": "An encyclopedic overview defining the Riemann Hypothesis as the conjecture that all non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function lie on the critical line Re(s)=1/2. It outlines the function's analytic properties, distinguishes trivial from non-trivial zeros, highlights its foundational role in prime number distribution, and notes its status as an unsolved Millennium Prize Problem supported by extensive numerical verification but lacking formal proof.", "limits": "Lines 1-537; scope confined to definitional exposition, historical context, Euler product formulation, and established mathematical significance."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86", "summary": "A comprehensive survey tracing 165 years of analytical, geometric, and physical approaches to the Riemann Hypothesis. It introduces an original method inspired by Riemann’s conformal mapping techniques, utilizing modern computation to extremize a quadratic form for highly accurate zero approximations that provably reside on the critical line. The work contextualizes these findings within algebraic geometry, automorphic forms, and quantum chaos, while proposing a future proof strategy based on trace formulas and the convergence of finite to infinite Euler products.", "limits": "Lines 1-506; scope bounded by historical survey, original computational/geometric derivations, and theoretical proof pathways without claiming resolution."}, {"source_id": "source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7", "summary": "A curated reference listing classical and advanced reformulations of the Riemann Hypothesis, including bounds on Mertens’ function and Chebyshev’s prime-counting function. It compiles academic equivalences spanning error terms in the Prime Number Theorem, Riesz’s and Li’s criteria, Bombieri’s positivity condition, Robin’s and Nicolas’ number-theoretic inequalities, and dynamical systems approaches involving Farey sequences, providing direct citations to foundational journal articles.", "limits": "Lines 1-389; scope limited to cataloging specific analytical, arithmetic, and geometric reformulations with academic citations."}]}, "review": {"findings": [{"check_id": "RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH", "severity": "major", "evidence": [{"id": "source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92", "locator": "{\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 1117}", "sha256": "904e6c045bec741cd71576fd5ba723ef07ebe87dca714ebc538529ae804f7d6d"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379", "locator": "{\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 537}", "sha256": "c435a5c8b3d5e4505b90a90478ab4be82af076cc7c4220d073eec0e920c5fc8f"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86", "locator": "{\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 506}", "sha256": "5d82f50a6f3928cf623501ddc49e4219e804461b6c603f05bedb5438ef6af263"}, {"id": "source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7", "locator": "{\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 389}", "sha256": "1784ec0de0fffc1bd637edf456e996983410a5b4ad64fc5deb9a6501ce6f7"}], "recommendation": "Replace generic reference materials with targeted theoretical analyses on the limits of empirical verification versus formal proof in analytic number theory, or explicitly frame the current compilation as a preliminary inventory pending rigorous epistemological analysis."}], "summary": "The direction mandates an explicit demonstration of the epistemological gap between finite computational verification and infinite mathematical proof regarding the Riemann Hypothesis. However, all provided sources are descriptive catalogs, encyclopedic overviews, or historical surveys that list equivalent formulations without analyzing the structural or logical barriers to empirical proof. Consequently, the artifact set does not support the directional objective, necessitating a major revision of the source material to include rigorous theoretical treatments of verification limits."}}, "claims": {"claims": [{"node_id": "node:051d4df2314b806eb7e85cf5303e38e8", "text": "Finite computational verification establishes a bound on the first 50 non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function, confirming they reside on the critical line Re(s)=1/2 with approximation errors not exceeding 10^-3, yet this empirical data cannot bridge the infinite gap to constitute a formal proof.", "kind": "proof_boundary", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86", "source-snapshot:d5898f1f19b5819dfe8f24dc4616d996ea502f9f17fe9a80ba8fa326bac39379"], "scope": "analytic number theory / computational verification limits", "rebuttal": {"claim_index": 0, "challenge": "Mischaracterizes Connes' quadratic form optimization as direct computational verification and conflates high-precision numerical candidates with rigorous analytic bounds. The 'infinite gap' is not merely a matter of finite versus infinite enumeration but stems from the absence of uniform analytic control over the critical strip at arbitrary heights.", "repair": "Reframe to specify that numerical approximations yield high-accuracy candidates on the critical line but cannot substitute for analytic continuation arguments required to exclude off-line zeros universally, emphasizing the necessity of uniform bounds across all imaginary parts rather than discrete verification."}}, {"node_id": "node:33b01e21dc5e6aa026ac18fa109ca893", "text": "The Riemann Hypothesis remains an unresolved mathematical question because the structural barrier between finite Euler product convergence and infinite analytic continuation prevents any single equivalent formulation—spanning algebraic geometry, automorphic forms, or random matrix theory—from yielding a definitive proof without entirely new foundational tools.", "kind": "proof_boundary", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:3bde46b4d14678c5d22dcdff1911f73eca4e5d4427aba2814319b333ed34cd92", "source-snapshot:3f9c88e8b8edfca544b18b213c6512dce6045086b68918c6764ed70174bddc86"], "scope": "mathematical logic / proof theory", "rebuttal": {"claim_index": 1, "challenge": "Overstates the necessity of 'entirely new foundational tools.' Established frameworks like trace formulas, automorphic forms, and spectral theory are mature but lack a proven self-adjoint operator whose spectrum matches the zeta zeros. The barrier is structural (missing spectral correspondence) rather than purely logical or axiomatic.", "repair": "Clarify that resolution hinges on establishing a concrete spectral interpretation within existing geometric and analytic frameworks, focusing on constructing operators with the correct eigenvalue distribution and refining trace formula applications instead of invoking new axioms or disjoint foundational systems."}}, {"node_id": "node:ec56dd793c9a110d98c698283bdf7c70", "text": "Any viable axiomatic extension proposed to resolve the Riemann Hypothesis must satisfy a strict bound on prime-counting error terms, specifically requiring that psi(x) - x = o(x^(1/2+epsilon)) holds uniformly, as current reformulations tie the hypothesis's validity directly to these asymptotic constraints rather than finite case enumeration.", "kind": "bound", "source_ids": ["source-snapshot:e2f0a42ab6cabf2a58c5b62698a7fee938f2870202fab84d035aea55b01ce6f7"], "scope": "number theory / asymptotic analysis", "rebuttal": {"claim_index": 2, "challenge": "Incorrectly posits that resolving RH requires an 'axiomatic extension,' whereas RH is widely expected to be provable within standard ZFC set theory. The equivalence involving psi(x) is a classical reformulation, not a foundational barrier, and framing it as such misdirects efforts toward unnecessary set-theoretic additions rather than analytic refinements.", "repair": "Replace 'axiomatic extension' with 'analytic refinement,' specifying that progress requires leveraging existing equivalences to derive uniform error bounds through trace formulas or modular forms within standard arithmetic geometry, avoiding speculative foundational shifts while targeting precise asymptotic control."}}], "rebuttal": {"rebuttals": [{"claim_index": 0, "challenge": "Mischaracterizes Connes' quadratic form optimization as direct computational verification and conflates high-precision numerical candidates with rigorous analytic bounds. The 'infinite gap' is not merely a matter of finite versus infinite enumeration but stems from the absence of uniform analytic control over the critical strip at arbitrary heights.", "repair": "Reframe to specify that numerical approximations yield high-accuracy candidates on the critical line but cannot substitute for analytic continuation arguments required to exclude off-line zeros universally, emphasizing the necessity of uniform bounds across all imaginary parts rather than discrete verification."}, {"claim_index": 1, "challenge": "Overstates the necessity of 'entirely new foundational tools.' Established frameworks like trace formulas, automorphic forms, and spectral theory are mature but lack a proven self-adjoint operator whose spectrum matches the zeta zeros. The barrier is structural (missing spectral correspondence) rather than purely logical or axiomatic.", "repair": "Clarify that resolution hinges on establishing a concrete spectral interpretation within existing geometric and analytic frameworks, focusing on constructing operators with the correct eigenvalue distribution and refining trace formula applications instead of invoking new axioms or disjoint foundational systems."}, {"claim_index": 2, "challenge": "Incorrectly posits that resolving RH requires an 'axiomatic extension,' whereas RH is widely expected to be provable within standard ZFC set theory. The equivalence involving psi(x) is a classical reformulation, not a foundational barrier, and framing it as such misdirects efforts toward unnecessary set-theoretic additions rather than analytic refinements.", "repair": "Replace 'axiomatic extension' with 'analytic refinement,' specifying that progress requires leveraging existing equivalences to derive uniform error bounds through trace formulas or modular forms within standard arithmetic geometry, avoiding speculative foundational shifts while targeting precise asymptotic control."}]}, "review": {"findings": [{"check_id": "PROOF-OBLIGATION-GAP", "claim_index": 0, "severity": "major", "analysis": "Fails to specify the precise analytic obligation required to bridge the empirical-to-formal gap. The claim reduces the 'infinite gap' to a matter of finite versus infinite enumeration, omitting the necessity of uniform analytic bounds across all imaginary parts to exclude off-line zeros universally.", "remediation": "Reframe to emphasize that high-precision numerical candidates cannot substitute for analytic continuation arguments, explicitly stating the obligation to derive uniform bounds via trace formulas or modular forms."}, {"check_id": "THEOREM-SCOPE-DRIFT", "claim_index": 1, "severity": "major", "analysis": "Drifts from established analytic number theory into speculative foundational territory by asserting that 'entirely new foundational tools' are necessary. The actual barrier is structural (missing spectral correspondence/self-adjoint operators), not axiomatic or logical.", "remediation": "Restrict scope to existing geometric and analytic frameworks. Clarify that resolution hinges on constructing concrete spectral interpretations and refining trace formula applications within mature theories like automorphic forms."}, {"check_id": "ASSUMPTION-SMUGGLE", "claim_index": 1, "severity": "moderate", "analysis": "Smuggles the assumption that equivalent formulations function as isolated structural barriers preventing proof. Logically, equivalent statements share identical truth conditions; proving one necessitates proving all, making the framing of 'single equivalent formulation' barriers logically incoherent.", "remediation": "Replace barrier language with logical interchangeability. Focus analysis on why current analytic techniques fail to establish the required uniform estimates rather than attributing failure to isolated reformulations."}, {"check_id": "ASSUMPTION-SMUGGLE", "claim_index": 2, "severity": "major", "analysis": "Illegitimately assumes that resolving RH requires an 'axiomatic extension,' contradicting the overwhelming consensus that the hypothesis is resolvable within standard ZFC set theory. Misframes a classical asymptotic equivalence as a foundational prerequisite.", "remediation": "Replace 'axiomatic extension' with 'analytic refinement.' Specify that progress requires leveraging existing equivalences to derive uniform error bounds through trace formulas or modular forms without invoking speculative set-theoretic additions."}]}}, "experiment": null}}}
{"type": "attempt_output", "content": {"output": {"title": "Riemann Hypothesis Proof Boundaries and Structural Barriers", "learned": ["The Riemann Hypothesis encompasses a dense network of mathematically equivalent formulations spanning analytic number theory, algebraic geometry, and spectral theory.", "High-precision numerical approximations confirm zeros reside on the critical line but cannot substitute for the uniform analytic bounds required to exclude off-line zeros universally.", "The core obstacle to resolution is structural—specifically the absence of a proven self-adjoint operator whose spectrum matches the zeta zeros—rather than axiomatic or purely logical."], "evidence": ["Comprehensive catalogs of equivalent statements including Mertens function bounds, Chebyshev psi error terms, Robin's and Nicolas' inequalities, and Farey sequence properties.", "Historical and theoretical surveys detailing 165 years of approaches, including Connes' quadratic form optimization yielding highly accurate critical-line candidates.", "Established mathematical connections between zeta zeros, prime distribution error terms, automorphic forms, and random matrix theory pair correlations."], "limitations": ["Provided sources are descriptive compilations and surveys lacking rigorous epistemological analysis of why finite verification fails to constitute formal proof.", "Cannot demonstrate that empirical zero-counting bridges the infinite gap, as the gap stems from missing uniform analytic control across arbitrary heights in the critical strip.", "Does not support claims requiring new axiomatic extensions, as the hypothesis is widely expected to be resolvable within standard ZFC set theory using existing frameworks."], "open_questions": ["How can a concrete self-adjoint operator be constructed within existing geometric or analytic frameworks to match the nontrivial zeta zeros?", "Which refinement of trace formulas or modular forms will yield the necessary uniform error bounds for prime-counting functions across all imaginary parts?", "How do the diverse equivalent formulations structurally interlock to reveal a unified proof pathway without relying on speculative foundational shifts?"], "next_moves": ["Replace broad equivalence catalogs with targeted theoretical analyses focusing on spectral correspondence and trace formula convergence mechanisms.", "Develop rigorous analytic arguments establishing uniform bounds on prime-counting error terms within standard arithmetic geometry.", "Investigate the construction of operators with correct eigenvalue distributions using mature theories like automorphic forms and étale cohomology.", "Evaluate specific classical reformulations (e.g., Li's criterion, Weil's positivity) for actionable structural insights rather than treating them as isolated proof barriers."]}, "tool_receipts": []}}
